        <p class="profile-detail-region">Ideology · Central &amp; East Asia</p>
        <h1 class="profile-detail-name">Legalism</h1>
        <div class="info-stats" style="margin-top:10px;">
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Period</span><span class="info-stat-val">c. 350 BCE – c. 200 CE</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Thinkers</span><span class="info-stat-val"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shang-yang">Shang Yang</a>, reformer and chancellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-buhai">Shen Buhai</a>, chancellor of the Han state and theorist of administrative technique (shu); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-dao">Shen Dao</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jixia-academy">Jixia Academy</a>, on positional power (shi); <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-fei">Han Fei</a>, considered by many the greatest Legalist thinker; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-kui">Li Kui</a>, an early author on regulations who influenced Shang Yang; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/li-si">Li Si</a>, praised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hu-shih">Hu Shih</a> with Han Fei as one of the greatest statesmen in Chinese history</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Key texts</span><span class="info-stat-val">the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-lord-shang">Book of Lord Shang</a> (Shangjunshu), possibly in broad circulation in the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warring-states-period">Warring States period</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-fei">Han Feizi</a>; the fragments of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-buhai">Shen Buhai</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-dao">Shen Dao</a>; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guanzi">Guanzi</a>, a late compilation reclassified from Daoism in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sui-dynasty">Sui dynasty</a>; Sima Tan’s essay On the Essentials of the Six Schools of Thought, which named the fa school</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Core ideas</span><span class="info-stat-val">rule by clear, public laws and standards (fa), with certain rewards and inevitable punishments; techniques of rule (shu) and the matching of performance to title (xing-ming); positional power (shi); equality under the law and appointment by merit rather than birth; agriculture and war as the state’s first concerns; a ruler practising non-action (wu wei) above universal standards; changing with the times rather than imitating antiquity</span></div>
          <div class="info-stat"><span class="info-stat-label">Today</span><span class="info-stat-val">no living school; cited by scholars and commentators as an influence on the governance of the People’s Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, particularly under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xi-jinping">Xi Jinping</a> (“<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> on the outside, Legalist on the inside”, in Sam Crane’s phrase); a label that many modern scholars avoid or regard as inaccurate</span></div>
