    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Legalism is a name first given in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-dynasty">Han dynasty</a> to a group of Warring States thinkers, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shang-yang">Shang Yang</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-buhai">Shen Buhai</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shen-dao">Shen Dao</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-fei">Han Fei</a>, who sought a strong state built on clear laws, administrative method, agriculture and war.</li><li>Its ideas were first put into practice in the state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shang-yang">Shang Yang</a>’s reforms in the 4th century BCE, which became instrumental in Qin’s conquest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> and the founding of its first imperial dynasty.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>’s reputation for brutality and harsh laws led later dynasties to proclaim <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/confucianism">Confucianism</a> instead, but the bureaucracy Qin built was adopted by the Han and shaped Chinese statecraft throughout Chinese history.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Despite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a>’s reputation for harsh laws, its officials could not punish without a confession and had to carry out forensic examinations; a beating, allowed only as a last resort, had to be reported and compared with the evidence.</p></aside>
    </section>

